WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
You finished the work, and in the meeting nobody said your name — and neither did you.

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:16.100
One voice inside you said "it's better this way"; another whispered "you let yourself be walked over again." So which of them is virtue, and which is cowardice in polite dress?

3
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.350
Picture a meeting.

4
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:35.120
A project that has taken months is finally finished, everyone around the table is pleased, and someone turns to thank the person who carried the real weight of it.

5
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.330
What does that person say?

6
00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:45.300
Almost always the same thing: oh, don't be silly, it was nothing, the team did the work, I just sketched a few things.

7
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.670
Then the meeting ends.

8
00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:57.240
Two weeks pass, and the same person walks into a salary negotiation, where the individual across the table asks: what exactly do you bring to this company?

9
00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.130
Now what will they say?

10
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:10.460
Someone who, half an hour earlier, could let the sentence "I didn't do anything" fall out of their mouth so easily — why do they suddenly begin to sweat when they have to answer that question?

11
00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.430
Turkish is one of the richest languages in the world on this subject.

12
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:22.560
We have a whole arsenal at the ready for deflecting praise: heaven forbid, oh come now, you're exaggerating, you're embarrassing me.

13
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:29.250
Nobody explains what these formulas are for while we are learning them; we only see what happens when they go unused.

14
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:35.460
And there is a ready-made adjective waiting for the person who doesn't use them: full of himself, pleased with herself.

15
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.430
Notice how heavy an accusation that phrase carries in Turkish.

16
00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:43.720
To be pleased by something is an innocent verb.

17
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.210
You can be pleased by a shirt, pleased by a view.

18
00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:51.660
But the moment you are pleased by yourself, the verb turns to poison.

19
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.270
The word for humility in Turkish, tevazu, tells us something too.

20
00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:02.800
It comes from the Arabic root wad', meaning to put, to lower, to place something further down.

21
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.730
By the very structure of the word, humility means placing yourself lower.

22
00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:11.700
And here is the crux: the word does not say how much lower.

23
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.910
One step?

24
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.640
Two?

25
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.170
All the way down under someone's feet?

26
00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.380
There is no measure.

27
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:27.390
And a virtue with no measure always ends up, in practice, in the hands of whichever party demands the most of it.

28
00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:31.520
Today's psychology has measured both ends of this ambiguity.

29
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:43.010
In 1999, Justin Kruger and David Dunning looked at how people assess their own competence and noticed something: those who knew the least in a given field predicted their own performance in the most inflated terms.

30
00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:49.060
As knowledge increased, what grew was not confidence but awareness of one's own limits.

31
00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.990
That finding led us to read humility almost as a sign of intelligence.

32
00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:57.480
The certain are suspect; the hesitant are deep.

33
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.930
But the other side of the coin has been on the table since 1978.

34
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:13.900
When Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes worked with some 150 women who were plainly successful in academia and professional life, they found that most of these women did not regard their achievements as their own.

35
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:19.630
It was luck, it was timing, someone had made a mistake, and soon they would be found out.

36
00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:23.920
In their own words, there was a feeling of being an impostor.

37
00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.210
These people were measuring themselves not as too much but as too little.

38
00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.860
And notice: nobody was penalizing them for it.

39
00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.110
On the contrary, the people around them probably loved them for how modest they seemed.

40
00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.440
This is where a logical error announces itself.

41
00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:47.130
If measuring yourself as more than you are is a mistake, why is measuring yourself as less than you are not a mistake?

42
00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:52.700
The virtue of an act of measurement must lie in its producing the correct result.

43
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:59.710
If we criticize a tailor for cutting too much cloth and applaud him for cutting too little, the subject is no longer tailoring.

44
00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:08.640
So our question is simple, and uncomfortable: is humility telling the truth about yourself, or telling less than the truth about yourself?

45
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.730
Because these two are not the same thing, and we give them the same name.

46
00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:19.460
The first person to set up a proper scale and weigh this question seriously was Aristotle.

47
00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:29.990
In the fourth book of the Nicomachean Ethics, he treats the matter of assigning value to oneself as a heading under virtue, and describes exactly how character goes wrong at this point.

48
00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:35.080
The order he builds is this: every virtue is a mean between two vices.

49
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.370
Courage is the mean between cowardice and recklessness.

50
00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.340
Generosity is the mean between stinginess and wastefulness.

51
00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:47.630
So what is the mean in assigning yourself your own worth?

52
00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:51.920
Aristotle calls it greatness of soul, magnanimity.

53
00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.650
The condition of a person who claims great worth for himself and genuinely deserves that worth.

54
00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:00.660
There is a deviation on either side.

55
00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.710
One claims a worth he has not earned: the vain man, the type we call arrogant.

56
00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:12.600
The other fails to claim the worth he has earned: in Aristotle's phrase, the small-souled man.

57
00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.570
And now comes the most startling sentence in the text.

58
00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:24.220
Aristotle says that smallness of soul is more opposed to greatness of soul than vanity is, because it is both more common and worse.

59
00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:26.270
Worse.

60
00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:32.960
A sentence written 2300 years ago turns all of our social reflexes upside down.

61
00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.170
Why should it be worse?

62
00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:41.860
Aristotle's reasoning is not moral but almost practical: the small-souled man does not use what he has.

63
00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:52.230
He does not reach for the work he is suited to, does not ask for the position he has earned, does not do the fine thing he is capable of, because he does not consider himself fit for it.

64
00:05:52.230 --> 00:06:01.960
The arrogant man at least attempts something, in the wrong place, and makes a fool of himself; the small-souled man attempts nothing anywhere, and nobody notices.

65
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.610
The first error is loud, the second is silent.

66
00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:14.220
It is entirely understandable that a society should punish the loud error and reward the silent one, but that does not turn the second error into a virtue.

67
00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:23.950
Aristotle goes far enough in describing the magnanimous man to raise a smile: he walks slowly, his voice is deep, he speaks without haste.

68
00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:28.880
That portrait looks too self-assured to us today, even slightly comic.

69
00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.050
But Aristotle does not leave the matter there.

70
00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:39.060
In the same book, he treats the way a person speaks about himself as a separate virtue, and calls it truthfulness.

71
00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:44.950
On one side stands the boaster, who describes himself as having what he does not have.

72
00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:50.520
On the other stands the self-deprecator, who describes himself as lacking what he does have.

73
00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:56.570
Socrates, with his famous posture of knowing nothing, is offered as an example of the second class.

74
00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.780
And in the middle?

75
00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:03.710
Neither more nor less: the person who tells the truth about himself.

76
00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:11.680
Which is to say that for Aristotle it is impossible for humility to conflict with honesty, because humility is a branch of honesty itself.

77
00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:19.650
And he adds a very fine warning: the person who goes to excess in belittling himself ends up boasting again.

78
00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.820
He is saying that plainness can turn into display.

79
00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:29.830
He has noticed that putting on the barest costume can, in front of the right audience, be a stronger claim than the most ornate one.

80
00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:34.120
That observation stands like a warning sign over this whole question.

81
00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.970
Did Aristotle's scale disappear?

82
00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:41.740
No, it was rebuilt in the Islamic world, and in a sharper form.

83
00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:53.070
In the 11th century, Ibn Miskawayh carried the doctrine of the mean into Arabic thought in his work Tahdhib al-Akhlaq, The Refinement of Character, and when he attached the virtues to their main headings,

84
00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:59.600
he placed humility among the sub-virtues of iffa, of temperance, of mastery over one's own appetites.

85
00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:07.410
In The Refinement of Character, virtue stands, just as in its Greek source, as a balance held between two extremes.

86
00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:11.220
But the clearest formula comes from al-Ghazali.

87
00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:22.550
In his great work Ihya Ulum al-Din, The Revival of the Religious Sciences, in the section where he takes up arrogance and self-conceit, he says this: humility is the mean between arrogance and mahana.

88
00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:27.000
Mahana means lowering yourself, fitting yourself out with abjectness.

89
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.890
So for al-Ghazali, placing yourself too high and placing yourself too low are both vices.

90
00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:35.420
Neither one is a virtue.

91
00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:43.550
It is worth pausing over the fact that a moralist speaking in religious language writes down the state of throwing yourself on the ground as a defect.

92
00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:48.480
Because it means humility was never one of those things where more is always better.

93
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.090
Mercy is like that; justice is like that; humility is not.

94
00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:56.900
An excess of humility is not humility but something else.

95
00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:02.150
Al-Ghazali makes one further distinction, and it moves the whole question somewhere new.

96
00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:09.800
For him, at the core of arrogance lies self-conceit, and the essence of self-conceit is claiming as your own something that does not belong to you.

97
00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.010
You did not make your voice.

98
00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.860
You did not build your intelligence.

99
00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:22.990
You did not arrange the city you were born in, the people who put you through school, the opportunity that appeared in front of you at the critical moment.

100
00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:29.520
If so, humility is not denying what you hold; it is knowing correctly where what you hold came from.

101
00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:33.970
This is not a question of quantity but a question of ownership.

102
00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:37.460
You have a talent; but it is not your invention.

103
00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:42.710
Erasing the first half of that sentence is not humility, merely bad accounting.

104
00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:46.360
Now a sledgehammer comes down on this whole delicate scale.

105
00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:49.530
What if there is no such virtue as humility?

106
00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:56.380
What if the centuries spent searching for that measure were nothing but an effort to dress a weakness in the costume of a virtue?

107
00:09:56.380 --> 00:10:04.350
In the 18th century, David Hume draws up a list in An Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals, published in 1751.

108
00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:11.200
Fasting, penance, mortification, self-denial, humility, silence, solitude.

109
00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:22.050
He calls these the monkish virtues, and explains in cold terms why they do not count as virtues: they neither improve a person's condition in the world nor make him a more valuable member of society.

110
00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:27.460
For Hume, these states dull the understanding, harden the heart, and sour the temper.

111
00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:34.310
So he takes them out of the column of virtues and writes them into the opposite column, the list of vices.

112
00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:36.520
Humility included.

113
00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:39.050
Hume has a positive side as well.

114
00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:47.180
In the third book of A Treatise of Human Nature, in the section where he takes up greatness of mind, he defends the usefulness of a measured pride.

115
00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:55.310
He says that a person who knows his own worth does his work better, that others trust him, that a confident bearing gives courage to those around him.

116
00:10:55.310 --> 00:11:00.880
Excessive humility, for Hume, is a defect; it gets in the way of getting things done.

117
00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:04.690
But in the same place Hume makes a very shrewd distinction.

118
00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:11.380
It is useful to carry pride inside you, he says; throwing it in someone else's face is not pleasant.

119
00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:15.830
Nobody enjoys being forced to acknowledge another person's superiority.

120
00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:20.120
Social life therefore needs an outward language of humility.

121
00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.970
Do you see what Hume has just done?

122
00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:27.100
He has taken humility out of morality and installed it in manners.

123
00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:30.750
Not a virtue, but courtesy.

124
00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:34.080
It will not be in you; it will be in your behavior.

125
00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:39.330
That is the precise philosophical description of our own deflecting formulas.

126
00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:43.140
Nietzsche, for his part, has no interest in courtesy.

127
00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:54.310
The well-known scene he describes in On the Genealogy of Morality, written in 1887, is this: lambs hate birds of prey and declare them evil; there is nothing strange in that.

128
00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:58.920
What is strange is that the lambs begin to count their own lamb-ness as goodness.

129
00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:06.090
Nietzsche's claim is that some part of our moral values is born from making it look as though we do not want to do the thing we cannot do.

130
00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:14.220
The condition of the one who has no power to strike back is called forgiveness, the condition of the one who must bow is called the virtue of obedience,

131
00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:18.830
and the condition of the one who cannot put himself forward is called humility.

132
00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:22.640
Weakness rewrites its own weakness as a choice.

133
00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:31.570
The most uncomfortable part of Nietzsche's suspicion is this: the person who descends the furthest may in fact have secured a position no one can compete for.

134
00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:40.340
The sentence "I am nobody" is also a claim, and it immediately places the listener under an obligation: no, don't say that.

135
00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.990
Literature diagnosed this before philosophy did.

136
00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:56.760
In the novel David Copperfield, which Charles Dickens completed in 1850, there is a character named Uriah Heep; the words "I am a humble person" never leave his mouth, his hands are forever wringing each other,

137
00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.450
he belittles himself without pause.

138
00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:03.260
That humility is not a disposition but a climbing tool.

139
00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:08.190
Dickens's novel can be read as a manual showing what false humility looks like.

140
00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.240
We all know this type.

141
00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:14.850
The person who refuses praise but repeats the refusal three times.

142
00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:19.940
The person who runs himself down so thoroughly that the other party is compelled to praise him.

143
00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:29.190
The person who escapes responsibility by saying "I'm useless at this anyway," because nobody demands an account from someone who has declared himself incompetent in advance,

144
00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:34.280
which is why belittling yourself can sometimes be the most comfortable shelter in the world.

145
00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:42.890
And the subtlest of them: the person who has turned humility into an identity, and who becomes angrier than anyone when that identity is touched.

146
00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:45.900
So is there no limit to this objection?

147
00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.110
There is.

148
00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:55.920
Nietzsche catches hold of something real, but spreading what he sees over everything means fitting one diagnosis to every patient.

149
00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:03.090
That a counterfeit of a virtue is in circulation does not show that the genuine article does not exist; it shows the opposite.

150
00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:05.940
Nobody counterfeits something worthless.

151
00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:12.310
Uriah Heep gets ahead by imitating humility precisely because humility carries genuine prestige out there.

152
00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:17.560
And so the real question becomes: what is the true form of a thing whose imitation is this easy?

153
00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:21.050
Let us come to the answer through the back door of language.

154
00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:27.420
The words for humility in Western languages come from the Latin humilitas, and that in turn comes from humus.

155
00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.270
Humus means soil, earth.

156
00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:36.160
So humility, in the memory of the word itself, means being close to the ground.

157
00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:39.970
The first thing that brings to mind is being trodden on.

158
00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.980
But consider what soil actually is.

159
00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.830
The thing that is stepped on, yes.

160
00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:49.160
But also the thing everything comes out of.

161
00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:55.210
Wheat comes out of the soil, trees come out of the soil, everyone we have lost goes into the soil.

162
00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:58.860
Soil is not worthless; it is fertile.

163
00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:06.670
Lying underfoot does not make it barren; on the contrary, lying beneath everything is exactly what makes it the source of everything.

164
00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:13.840
The border between abjectness and humility stands right here, and to my mind the whole question is settled in this single distinction.

165
00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:18.770
The person who casts himself into abjectness considers himself garbage.

166
00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:22.100
The humble person considers himself soil.

167
00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:26.550
Nothing grows out of garbage; soil yields the entire harvest.

168
00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:30.840
Both are down below; one is empty, the other full.

169
00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:40.890
Seen this way, the line al-Ghazali draws between arrogance and abjectness is not merely a proposal about balance but a difference between two entirely separate states of soul.

170
00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:46.460
Anatolian thought settled this difference not with concepts but with a single line of verse.

171
00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:54.910
That line of Yunus Emre's that everyone knows is in fact a definition of humility: knowledge is knowing knowledge, knowledge is knowing yourself.

172
00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:56.960
Knowing yourself.

173
00:15:56.960 --> 00:16:00.930
Not shrinking yourself, not enlarging yourself; knowing.

174
00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:04.260
He has reduced the work of the scale to a single verb.

175
00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:10.950
And the same poet has a far deeper line: there is an I within me, further in than me.

176
00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:15.240
He is saying there is a layer inside us deeper than the self we know.

177
00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.250
Humility means making room for that layer.

178
00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:23.340
Drawing the outer self aside, so that what lies behind it can be seen.

179
00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.990
Rumi's Masnavi opens with exactly this image.

180
00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:32.720
Listen to the reed, he says; it is complaining, because it was cut from the reed bed.

181
00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:37.010
The pain in the poet's opening couplet is a real pain.

182
00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:43.380
But consider: for the reed to be able to make a sound, it first had to be cut, and then hollowed out.

183
00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:46.710
A reed that is full inside makes no sound at all.

184
00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:50.200
That emptiness is not deprivation but a passage.

185
00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:58.970
And so humility too is not emptying yourself, not reducing yourself to nothing; it is making yourself into something that another thing can pass through.

186
00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:02.140
Stepping out of the way, not disappearing.

187
00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:12.190
Yunus has one more line, one that sorts out the counterfeit in a single stroke: if being a dervish were a matter of crown and cloak, we could buy one ourselves for thirty or forty.

188
00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:15.680
It does not work by costume.

189
00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:25.410
False humility has an outfit, a tone of voice, a wringing of hands; the true kind has none, because the true kind is not occupied with showing itself.

190
00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:31.780
Today's moral psychology, in trying to make this question measurable, has arrived at a similar place.

191
00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:38.950
There is a field of research called intellectual humility, and its criterion is not at all what you would expect.

192
00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:42.920
They do not look at how much a person disparages himself.

193
00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:48.170
What they look at is this: can this person change his mind in the face of new evidence?

194
00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:51.020
Can he say he was wrong?

195
00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:55.950
On a subject he does not know, can he say he does not know and stop there?

196
00:17:55.950 --> 00:18:01.040
Humility, in other words, is measured not as a feeling but as a behavior.

197
00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:05.970
Not how you feel about yourself, but what you do when you come up against the truth.

198
00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:11.220
And looked at from here, it turns out that the opposite of humility is not arrogance.

199
00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:19.670
The arrogant person and the person who thinks himself worthless are, very strangely, doing the same work: both are constantly looking at themselves.

200
00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:25.240
One with admiration, the other with disgust, but both are standing in front of the mirror.

201
00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:35.290
The person in a meeting checking whether his intelligence is being observed and the person who imagines everyone has noticed his mistake are both failing to look at the actual work in the room.

202
00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:38.620
Humility is lifting your head from the mirror.

203
00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:46.270
Taking your attention off yourself and turning it toward the work, toward the person in front of you, toward the problem that needs solving.

204
00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:51.680
Which is why its true opposite is not self-aggrandizement but being stuck on yourself.

205
00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:55.490
How this looks in daily life is perfectly clear.

206
00:18:55.490 --> 00:19:03.780
Is the better physician the doctor who tells you she is not certain of the diagnosis and wants to consult a colleague, or the one who never says she doesn't know?

207
00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:15.590
Is the more trustworthy teacher the one who says, faced with a student's question, "I never thought about it that way, let me look into it and come back," or the one who invents an answer for every question?

208
00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:24.520
Is the engineer who says "you're right, I'm doing it again from the start" when an error is found in his calculation the one taking his work seriously?

209
00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:27.690
None of these people is belittling himself.

210
00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:32.620
Each of them is stating his own limit, exactly where it lies, correctly.

211
00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:36.110
And that is precisely why they are trusted.

212
00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:43.280
This is where humility's prestige comes from: the word of a humble person is weighty because it matches the thing it is about.

213
00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:49.010
So let us return to the question we began with, and we can now split it in two.

214
00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:58.260
Being unfair to yourself means saying you did nothing when you did in fact do something, not asking for the work you have earned, not walking toward the place you deserve.

215
00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:00.950
That is not called humility.

216
00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:12.280
For Aristotle it is smallness of soul, for al-Ghazali it is casting yourself into abjectness, for Hume it is a useless monkish habit, and for Nietzsche it is weakness lying to itself.

217
00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:18.170
Four thinkers from four different languages say, on this point, astonishingly the same thing.

218
00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:28.700
Humility is the opposite operation: saying the truth as it stands, both what you can do and what you cannot, both what you have and what you lack, and then not speaking of it again.

219
00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:38.270
As for the matter of the scale, we have to admit something: Aristotle is asking the right question, but as long as we are the ones still holding the weights, the scale will tilt.

220
00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:42.080
There are not many people who weigh their own heft impartially.

221
00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:51.170
Perhaps the real solution is not to stop weighing but to hand the weighing over: to the work itself, to the result, to what the person across from you can see.

222
00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:56.740
To let the thing you have made speak about you, and to keep your own voice down while it speaks.

223
00:20:56.740 --> 00:21:04.710
The soil does not think little of itself, and it says nothing at all; it simply stays where it is, so that something can grow on top of it.

224
00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:08.520
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

225
00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:12.170
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
